course: biology
lesson: 390
title: How animals sleep: half-asleep dolphins, standing horses, short-sleeping giraffes and birds that nap in the air
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In an aquarium a dolphin swims slowly in circles with one eye shut. It is asleep, but only with half its brain: the other half stays awake to steer, come up to breathe and watch for danger.
explain: Every animal sleeps in a way that fits its life. Ducks at the ends of a row keep the outward eye open, frigatebirds nap for seconds while gliding over the ocean, and horses doze standing up so they can run if danger comes.
explain: Big plant-eaters like giraffes and elephants sleep only a short time, because they need many hours to eat and lying down can be dangerous with lions about.
explain: Animals that eat poor food, like koalas, or must wait for their food, like bats, sleep a lot to save energy.
explain: Some famous sleep facts are myths, because they came from animals in zoos, which have less to do and no danger.
fact: A dolphin can sleep with one half of its brain while the other half stays awake, and the halves take turns.
fact: Ducks at the edge of a sleeping row keep the outward eye open and that half of the brain awake, while ducks in the middle sleep more deeply.
fact: Frigatebirds can fly for weeks without landing; scientists found they slept less than an hour a day while flying, mostly in seconds-long naps, but about twelve hours a day on land.
fact: Horses can lock their legs and doze standing, but for their deepest sleep they must lie down for a short time.
fact: Wild giraffes sleep in short bits adding up to about half an hour to a few hours, often standing, because getting up takes them a long time.
fact: Scientists reported that wild sloths sleep about nine or ten hours a day, not twenty; the big number came from sloths in zoos.
fact: Wild elephants in Africa were reported to sleep only about two hours a night, mostly standing, and some nights not at all.
fact: Chinstrap penguins guarding nests were reported to take over ten thousand tiny naps a day, each a few seconds long, adding up to about eleven hours.
fact: Fish rest by slowing down and hovering still; they have no eyelids, so their eyes stay open, and some parrotfish sleep in a bubble of slime.
fact: Koalas sleep or rest about 18 to 22 hours a day, and some bats sleep about 20 hours.
fact: Sleeping a lot saves energy when food is poor or comes only at certain times.
exercise: How does a dolphin sleep? | With one half of its brain at a time | With its whole brain at the bottom of the sea | It never sleeps at all | Only on the beach
exercise: Why can't a dolphin fall fully asleep under water? | It must come up to breathe and watch for danger | It would float away up to the surface and dry out in the hot sun | Its fins would fall off | It would turn into a fish
exercise: Which ducks in a sleeping row keep one eye open? | The ones at the ends | The ones in the middle | Only the youngest babies | None of them at all, ever
exercise: Why do ducks in the middle sleep more deeply? | They are safer, with others around them | They are older | They are more tired from leading the flock all day | They have no eyes
exercise: How much did frigatebirds sleep while flying over the ocean? | Less than an hour a day, in tiny naps | Twelve hours straight, gliding on the wind | No sleep for a whole year | Twenty hours a day
exercise: How do horses doze standing up? | They lock their legs | They lean against a tree | They hang from a rope | They stand on two legs
exercise: Why must a horse lie down sometimes? | For its deepest sleep | To eat the grass below | To cool its hot hooves on the ground | To hear danger much better
exercise: How much do wild giraffes sleep? | About half an hour to a few hours, in short bits | About twelve hours | About twenty hours, lying flat on the warm ground | They never sleep
exercise: Why is lying down risky for a giraffe? | Getting up takes a long time, which is dangerous with lions about | Its long neck breaks if it lies down on the ground | It falls asleep for days | It cannot breathe lying down
exercise: How long do wild sloths sleep, scientists reported? | About nine or ten hours | About twenty hours | About one hour, in the afternoon | About two days
exercise: Why can zoo animals sleep more than wild ones? | They have less to do and no danger | Zoos give them sleeping pills in their food at night | Zoo nights are longer | They are lazier by birth
exercise: About how long do wild elephants sleep a night? | About two hours | About ten hours a night | About twenty hours | About six hours or so
exercise: How did chinstrap penguins guarding nests sleep? | In over ten thousand naps a few seconds long | In one long nap at night while their partner stands guard | Only in the summer | Never while guarding
exercise: Why can't a fish close its eyes? | Fish have no eyelids | Its eyes are too big to shut | Water keeps them open | Fish never rest at all
exercise: How long does a koala sleep or rest each day? | About 18 to 22 hours | Only about 2 hours a day | About 8 hours, like people | It never rests at all
exercise: Why might an animal that eats poor food sleep a lot? | Sleep saves energy | Poor food makes it sick | It forgets to wake up | It eats in its sleep
test: In Ice Age, Sid the sloth is lazy and sleepy. A friend says sloths sleep twenty hours a day. What is right? | Wild sloths were reported to sleep about nine or ten hours | Yes, twenty hours in the wild, even more than in the zoo | Sloths never sleep | Sloths sleep only one hour
test: Count them: a row of 6 ducks sleeps on a log, and the 2 at the ends each keep 1 eye open. How many eyes are open? | Two | Six | Twelve | Four
test: Count them: 3 horses doze, each resting one back hoof lifted off the ground. How many hooves are on the ground? | Nine | Twelve | Three | Six
test: In Tom and Jerry, Tom sleeps flat on his back snoring for hours. Which real animal often dozes standing up instead? | The horse | The house cat | The koala | The bat
test: «my goldfish is dead, it's just hanging there with its eyes open» What is probably happening? | It is resting; fish have no eyelids | It is definitely dead | It is staring at you because it wants food | It is holding its breath
test: What would happen if a giraffe lay down for a long sleep with lions nearby? | It would be slow to get up and in danger | It would jump up in a second | Lions never hunt giraffes | Nothing, giraffes sleep lying all night
test: «penguin naps of 4 seconds? that's not proper sleep lol» What is right? | Over ten thousand of them added up to about eleven hours | They do not count as sleep at all | Penguins sleep only in winter | Penguins nap for four hours each time, not four seconds as people say
test: «elephants sleep loads, they're huge» What is right? | Wild elephants were reported to sleep only about two hours a night | They sleep twenty hours | They sleep only in water | They sleep all winter in caves, like bears, because they are so big
test: Why would a bird sleep less while flying over the sea? | It must keep steering and watching, with nowhere safe to land | Seawater keeps it awake | It is never tired at sea | It sleeps more at sea
test: «koala sleeps all day, lazy git» What is the real reason? | Its leaves give little energy, so sleep saves energy | It is ill every day | It stays up all night partying | Koalas are lazy by choice and could be busy if they wanted to
test: Why do some bats sleep about twenty hours a day? | They rest when there are no insects to catch | They are afraid of the dark | They are always cold and stay asleep to keep warm | They hang upside down for fun
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sleep of dolphins. | One cerebral hemisphere sleeps while the other remains awake, the two alternating | Dolphins sleep with the entire brain at the bottom of the sea | Dolphins abstain from sleep entirely | Dolphins sleep only upon the shore
exam: Ascertain the approximate daily duration of sleep reported for wild elephants in Africa. | Two hours | Ten hours | Twenty hours | Six hours
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the duration of sleep attributed to sloths was formerly overstated. | Early observations were made upon animals kept in zoos | Sloths were observed only in winter | Sloths conceal themselves while awake | Observers confused sloths with koalas
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sleep of frigatebirds in flight? | They sleep less than an hour daily, chiefly in naps of a few seconds | They sleep twelve hours continuously while gliding in the air | They never sleep at any time | They sleep only upon the surface of the sea
